<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Rotelyx Chat</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Rotelyx Chat</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<!-- Export compliance.

	     TRUE, and this is not the cautious answer, it is the correct one.

	     The exemption most applications claim covers software that only calls
	     the encryption already in the operating system. This one does not: it
	     ships its own implementation of MLS and X-Wing in a Rust library, which
	     is exactly what the exemption excludes.

	     Declaring false here to skip the questionnaire is a false statement to
	     Apple and, through them, to the United States Bureau of Industry and
	     Security. What it actually requires is an annual self-classification
	     report, or a CCATS, before distribution. See docs/RELEASING.md, which
	     also covers the separate French declaration.
	-->
	<!-- Woken by a push, and by nothing else.

	     `remote-notification` is what lets the notification service extension
	     run when a content-free push arrives. There is no `fetch` and no
	     `processing`: this application does not poll in the background, because
	     iOS does not permit the socket that would make polling useful.

	     Notably absent: `audio` and `voip`. PushKit is the right mechanism for
	     an incoming call and the wrong one for a message, and Apple revokes the
	     entitlement of applications that use it for messages.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
	</array>
	<!-- What iOS shows the person when it asks.

	     These are not boilerplate. iOS refuses to launch an application that
	     touches the microphone or camera without them, and the text is what the
	     person reads while deciding, so it says what the thing is for and stops.

	     Notably absent: NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription. Attachments go through
	     the system document picker, which asks for nothing. Reading the photo
	     library would give a prettier grid and cost a line that a person reads
	     as "this app can see all my photographs".
	-->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rotelyx needs the microphone for voice calls. It is used only while a call is running.</string>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Rotelyx needs the camera to scan a meeting code. Nothing is recorded and nothing is sent.</string>
	<!-- ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption is deliberately absent, and absent is not
	     the same as false.

	     This application uses non-exempt encryption: it ships its own MLS and
	     X-Wing implementation rather than calling the operating system's, and
	     declaring false would be a false statement to Apple and, through them,
	     to the United States Bureau of Industry and Security. It is never to be
	     set false here.

	     Declaring true is the other half, and it requires the binary to carry
	     ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode, the code App Store Connect issues
	     against uploaded encryption documentation. Without a matching code the
	     upload is refused at validation with "the export compliance key value
	     [] doesn't match", which is what happened.

	     With the key absent, App Store Connect asks the export questions per
	     submission instead and they are answered there, truthfully. To go back
	     to declaring it in the binary, put both keys in:

	         <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key><true/>
	         <key>ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode</key><string>THE-CODE</string>

	     docs/EXPORT-COMPLIANCE.md holds the answers either path needs.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleSignature</key>
	<string>????</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSupportsIndirectInputEvents</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
